Everything looks solid, and you’ve correctly identified that @JsonRawValue in your CommonRequestDto is the key. However, for this to work seamlessly end-to-end, there are three specific areas where you might be running into issues.
The main culprit is usually that the Entity (CommonRequest) and the DTO (CommonRequestDto) need to be perfectly aligned in how they handle the payload string.
1. Update the Helper Mapper
In your searchLhoTransfers implementation, the mapToRequestDto helper is responsible for moving the data. You need to ensure it pulls the payload correctly.
File: CommonMasterServiceImpl.java (Inside the mapper method)
private CommonRequestDto mapToRequestDto(CommonRequest entity) {
    return CommonRequestDto.builder()
            .id(entity.getId())
            .requestType(entity.getRequestType())
            .changeType(entity.getChangeType())
            .status(entity.getStatus())
            .requestDate(entity.getRequestDate())
            .creatorId(entity.getCreatorId())
            .creatorRemarks(entity.getCreatorRemarks())
            .executionDate(entity.getExecutionDate())
            .executionRemarks(entity.getExecutionRemarks())
            .executorId(entity.getExecutorId())
            .executorRemarks(entity.getExecutorRemarks())
            // CRITICAL: Ensure this is the RAW JSON string from the DB
            .payload(entity.getPayload()) 
            .targetId(entity.getTargetId())
            .build();
}

2. The Entity Getter Fix
Even though you have @JsonRawValue on the DTO, if your Entity has a customized getPayload() that is doing anything weird (like extra escaping), it will break. Check your CommonRequest model.
File: CommonRequest.java (The Entity)
Ensure the getter is simple. If you have @JsonRawValue here too, it helps when debugging, but it's mandatory on the DTO.
@Lob
@Column(name = "PAYLOAD")
private String payload;

public String getPayload() {
    return this.payload; // Should return exactly: {"batchId": "..."}
}

3. Verification of the Controller Response
Your LhoTransferController returns ResponseEntity<ApiResponse<...>>. Ensure your ApiResponse wrapper isn't manually stringifying the data. Jackson should handle the whole object tree at once.
Why it might still show as a String (Troubleshooting)
If you still see backslashes in your API response after these changes, check these two things:
 * Double Serialization: Are you accidentally saving the JSON into the database as a string twice? If the database value looks like "{\"key\": \"value\"}" (with literal quotes at the start and end), Jackson will still treat the inner part as text. The database value should start with { and end with }.
 * DTO Import: Ensure your searchLhoTransfers is definitely using the CommonRequestDto from the package where you added @JsonRawValue.
Final Check on your CommonRequestDto
You have this correctly:
@JsonRawValue
private String payload;

This is 90% of the battle. When Spring sends this DTO through the MappingJackson2HttpMessageConverter, it sees @JsonRawValue and says: "I won't put quotes around this string. I'll just paste the text directly into the output."
